      "hints": [
        "If two lines are perpendicular, what is the product of their slopes?",
        "The x-intercept is where the line meets the x-axis, so put \\(y = 0\\)."
      ],
      "solution": {
        "modelAnswer": "(a) \\(m_{AB} = \\frac{-1 - 3}{5 - (-1)} = -\\tfrac{2}{3}\\).\n\n(b) Perpendicular slope \\(= \\tfrac{3}{2}\\). Through \\(C(2, 6)\\): \\(y - 6 = \\tfrac{3}{2}(x - 2) \\Rightarrow 2y - 12 = 3x - 6 \\Rightarrow 3x - 2y + 6 = 0\\).\n\n(c) Put \\(y = 0\\): \\(3x + 6 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = -2\\).",
        "finalAnswer": "(a) \\(-\\tfrac{2}{3}\\); (b) \\(3x - 2y + 6 = 0\\); (c) \\(-2\\)",
        "steps": [
          {
            "n": 1,
            "partKey": "a",
            "title": "Slope of AB",
            "expression": "m_{AB} = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} = \\frac{-1 - 3}{5 - (-1)} = \\frac{-4}{6}",
            "result": "m_{AB} = -\\tfrac{2}{3}",
            "explanation": "Use the slope formula with \\(A(-1, 3)\\) and \\(B(5, -1)\\).",
            "rule": "Slope \\(= \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}\\)",
            "diagramId": "fig1",
            "isFinal": true
          },
          {
            "n": 2,
            "partKey": "b",
            "title": "Slope of the perpendicular",
            "expression": "m \\times \\left(-\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right) = -1 \\Rightarrow m = \\tfrac{3}{2}",
            "explanation": "Perpendicular lines have slopes whose product is \\(-1\\): take the negative reciprocal.",
            "rule": "Perpendicular lines: \\(m_1 m_2 = -1\\)"
          },
          {
            "n": 3,
            "partKey": "b",
            "title": "Point-slope form through C",
            "expression": "y - 6 = \\tfrac{3}{2}(x - 2)",
            "explanation": "The line passes through \\(C(2, 6)\\) with slope \\(\\tfrac{3}{2}\\).",
            "rule": "\\(y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)\\)"
          },
          {
            "n": 4,
            "partKey": "b",
            "title": "Simplify",
            "expression": "2y - 12 = 3x - 6 \\Rightarrow 3x - 2y + 6 = 0",
            "result": "3x - 2y + 6 = 0",
            "explanation": "Multiply both sides by 2 and collect all terms on one side. Check: \\(C(2, 6)\\) gives \\(6 - 12 + 6 = 0\\).",
            "diagramId": "fig1",
            "isFinal": true
          },
          {
            "n": 5,
            "partKey": "c",
            "title": "x-intercept",
            "expression": "y = 0:\\ 3x + 6 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = -2",
            "result": "-2",
            "explanation": "On the x-axis \\(y = 0\\). The line crosses the x-axis at \\((-2, 0)\\).",
            "diagramId": "fig1",
            "isFinal": true
          }
        ],
        "commonMistakes": [
          "Using the reciprocal \\(-\\tfrac{3}{2}\\) without changing the sign, which gives a line that is not perpendicular.",
          "Writing the line through \\(A\\) or \\(B\\) instead of through \\(C\\).",
          "Finding the y-intercept (3) instead of the x-intercept in part (c)."
        ],
        "alternativeMethods": [
          {
            "title": "Slope-intercept form",
            "description": "In (b), write \\(y = \\tfrac{3}{2}x + c\\) and substitute \\(C(2, 6)\\): \\(6 = 3 + c\\), so \\(c = 3\\) and \\(y = \\tfrac{3}{2}x + 3\\), which is equivalent to \\(3x - 2y + 6 = 0\\)."
          }
        ]
